## Solution

**Answer-leak check:** The problem statement contains only the problem; no solution, sketch, or answer is embedded. Proceeding.

---

Let
$$L_n=\left(\frac{\prod_{k=1}^{n}\left(1+\dfrac{xk}{n^{2}}\right)}{\prod_{k=1}^{n}\left(1+\dfrac{x^{2}k^{2}}{n^{4}}\right)}\right)^{x/n},\qquad x>0.$$
We show $L_n\to 1$.

Take logarithms:
$$\ln L_n=\frac{x}{n}\left[\sum_{k=1}^{n}\ln\!\left(1+\frac{xk}{n^{2}}\right)-\sum_{k=1}^{n}\ln\!\left(1+\frac{x^{2}k^{2}}{n^{4}}\right)\right].$$

**Numerator sum.** Set $u_k=\dfrac{xk}{n^{2}}$. Since $\max_k u_k=\dfrac{x}{n}\to0$, we may use $\ln(1+u)=u+O(u^{2})$ uniformly in $k$:
$$\sum_{k=1}^{n}u_k=\frac{x}{n^{2}}\cdot\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=\frac{x(n+1)}{2n}=\frac{x}{2}+\frac{x}{2n},$$
$$\sum_{k=1}^{n}u_k^{2}\le n\cdot\left(\frac{x}{n}\right)^{2}=\frac{x^{2}}{n}\longrightarrow 0.$$
Hence
$$\sum_{k=1}^{n}\ln\!\left(1+\frac{xk}{n^{2}}\right)=\frac{x}{2}+o(1).$$

**Denominator sum.** Set $v_k=\dfrac{x^{2}k^{2}}{n^{4}}$. Since $\max_k v_k=\dfrac{x^{2}}{n^{2}}\to0$, again $\ln(1+v)=v+O(v^{2})$ uniformly:
$$\sum_{k=1}^{n}v_k=\frac{x^{2}}{n^{4}}\cdot\frac{n(n+1)(2n+1)}{6}=\frac{x^{2}(2n^{3}+O(n^{2}))}{6n^{4}}\longrightarrow 0,$$
$$\sum_{k=1}^{n}v_k^{2}\le n\cdot\left(\frac{x^{2}}{n^{2}}\right)^{2}=\frac{x^{4}}{n^{3}}\longrightarrow 0.$$
Hence
$$\sum_{k=1}^{n}\ln\!\left(1+\frac{x^{2}k^{2}}{n^{4}}\right)=o(1).$$

**Combine.** Therefore
$$\ln L_n=\frac{x}{n}\left[\frac{x}{2}+o(1)-o(1)\right]=\frac{x^{2}}{2n}+o\!\left(\frac{1}{n}\right)\longrightarrow 0.$$

By continuity of the exponential,
$$\lim_{n\to\infty}L_n=e^{0}=1.$$

